.TH GXWI-SD-EDITOR 1 "2026-10-05" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-sd-editor \- edit a security descriptor in a dialog on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-sd-editor
.SH DESCRIPTION
Opened by another program, which wants the security descriptor of something
of its own edited: a file, a registry key, a service. That program starts it
as a child, on the desktop it is on, and the two speak over the editor's
standard input and output, one JSON object a line. The program's first line
says what the object is called, what kind of thing it is, what its rights are
called, which parts of its descriptor it read, and what its descriptor is.
The editor shows it in a dialog of its own. When the person presses Apply or
OK, the editor writes the whole descriptor back, with the parts of it that
changed, and the program applies it and answers whether it could. The editor
changes nothing itself.
.PP
The whole descriptor is shown and edited. Access & Auditing lists everyone
the descriptor names; for whoever is picked, Access has a box for each general
right the program named, allowed and denied, where the rule applies on a
container, and the rights one at a time; Conditionals holds the rules that
apply only while a condition on claims or group membership holds; Specific
Rights holds rules for particular parts of the object, where the program
names its parts; and Auditing holds what is recorded. Claims are the object's
resource claims, and Central Access Policy the policies it names. Effective
Access works out what someone could do with it, and why, as KACS would. The
owner, primary group, integrity label and process trust label are in the
dropdown under the owner's name.
.PP
Advanced Mode shows the access list and the SACL entry by entry, as KACS reads
them, and changes, moves, duplicates and removes entries; and the descriptor's
owner, group, control flags and SDDL. An entry the other tabs cannot show is
kept where it was found, and shown here. An entry nobody changed goes back to
the program exactly as it came.
.PP
What the program cannot change is shown and not offered, and the dialog says
why; where it can change nothing, Close is the only button. The owner can be
given only to whom KACS allows: the person themselves, a group they may own
things as, or anyone with SeRestorePrivilege. Who the person is comes from the
editor's own token, which is the program's.
.PP
The claims conditions have tested are remembered in the machine's registry, at
Machine\\Software\\Peios\\Permissions Editor, for suggesting next time, by
whoever may write that key.
.PP
The editor ends when the dialog is closed or cancelled, and after OK once the
program has applied what it sent. It ends as well when its standard input
closes, and when the program that started it does.
.PP
Started by hand, with no request on its standard input, it says so and ends.
.SH SEE ALSO
The protocol is set out in the Peios userspace protocols (PSPU), on
learn.peios.org.
